## Migration law

1. Preserve the current implementation and its real consumers.
2. Fence the invariant and exact behavioral boundary.
3. Admit a closed graph/spec source.
4. Render with deterministic ggen frontmatter.
5. Run the generator twice and require a clean second diff.
6. Exercise positive, negative, mutation, and replay fixtures.
7. Remove hand-authored authority only after observed equivalence.

## Exclusions

This projection does not claim semantic equivalence, runtime execution,
performance improvement, subsystem closure, or that every high-scoring file
should become generated code. Algorithms remain hand-authored unless generation
preserves their object model, failure semantics, admission, and receipts.

## Falsifier

The generated Rust registry must fail if any admitted path is missing, duplicated,
or unsorted. The inventory receipt must refuse any source-tree or RDF drift.
